WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.000
The main antagonist of the DCEU's&nbsp;
Justice League is Steppenwolf,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.600
a dishonored general from Apokolips&nbsp;
who once failed to conquer Earth and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:12.240
as a punishment, was banished. He's back and&nbsp;
shinier than ever in the epic Snyder Cut.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:15.920
But what's his deal, anyway? Here's&nbsp;
the untold truth of Steppenwolf.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:20.240
Despite boasting such a major big screen&nbsp;
appearance, Steppenwolf has remained somewhat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:24.880
unknown to the general public, which is a shame&nbsp;
given his rich history in the source material.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:30.080
Steppenwolf first entered the DC scene in&nbsp;
1972 in the pages of Jack Kirby's New Gods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:33.040
two years after the debut of&nbsp;
his lord and master, Darkseid.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:36.800
Despite not being in charge of Apokolips,&nbsp;
Steppenwolf has proven important enough to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.720
survive multiple retcons, and has famously&nbsp;
appeared in two different forms on the big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:44.320
screen … or at least he will, if&nbsp;
the Snyder Cut ever hits theaters.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.200
"At last, you call me home!"

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.560
Wait a minute. Isn't Jack Kirby a Marvel&nbsp;
guy? What's he doing moonlighting over at DC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:57.440
inventing evil gods? Fair question. Kirby&nbsp;
felt a bit underappreciated at Marvel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:00.560
what with Stan Lee's increasing&nbsp;
celebrity. In an attempt to step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.760
out from his media-savvy partner's&nbsp;
shadow, he struck out on his own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:08.160
signing an exclusive three-year deal to work&nbsp;
his Kirby magic for the rival publisher.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:12.640
Eager to surpass Lee, Kirby wanted to go&nbsp;
big with his DC debut. He envisioned a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:16.560
massive expansion of the DC universe via&nbsp;
three interconnected series that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.640
introduce expansive science-fiction worlds&nbsp;
and technologies, as well as elements of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:24.880
popular pulp genres of the time like space&nbsp;
opera, to the world of Batman and Superman.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:27.520
Kirby named this project "Fourth&nbsp;
World," and with it he hoped to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.720
tell stories with existential&nbsp;
stakes on a galactic level.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:35.120
Steppenwolf and Darkseid were born of this&nbsp;
grandiose aspiration, and to be honest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:40.160
we're lucky they're still around, because Kirby's&nbsp;
master plan kinda fell flat for DC readers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:44.880
The Fourth World series was quickly and&nbsp;
unceremoniously canceled due to lack of sales.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:49.840
Kirby bid DC adieu and went back to Marvel&nbsp;
for a bit, before venturing forth into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:54.640
wild world of indie comics. Kirby did come back&nbsp;
to DC during the Reagan administration to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:58.960
to his destructive creations. He apparently&nbsp;
wanted to kill them all off definitively,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:04.240
but DC had attachment issues even then, and a&nbsp;
standing policy of denying the finality of death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:07.760
As a result, Steppenwolf and the other&nbsp;
New Gods got to stick around in various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:11.600
conflicting incarnations long after&nbsp;
their creator's time with them was done.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:15.760
Steppenwolf and Darkseid, along with the other&nbsp;
New Gods, have been perhaps the most enduring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:20.080
products of this brief sojourn for Kirby&nbsp;
across town. The real-world origin story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.840
of these characters goes a long way in&nbsp;
explaining their difficult birth into DC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:26.960
and the epic retconning that took&nbsp;
place for decades thereafter.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:31.840
Steppenwolf is one of the New Gods, a race&nbsp;
of beings who reside on the twin planets of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:36.160
Apokolips and New Genesis, created by&nbsp;
Jack Kirby in the '70s during Kirby's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:40.960
brief-but-passionate affair with rival comics&nbsp;
shop DC. There's not much moral complexity in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:45.360
New Gods. In the Fourth World stories,&nbsp;
New Genesis is good; Apokolips is bad.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:50.000
New Genesis is a futuristic utopia at peace&nbsp;
with itself, and brimming with astonishing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.120
technology. It basically looks like the future we&nbsp;
were promised in the 1950s. Apokolips, meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:59.600
looks more like the future we actually got.&nbsp;
Other Apokoliptian New Gods include such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:04.400
fascistic residents as Darkseid and Granny&nbsp;
Goodness, and like most godly pantheons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:08.080
all of these characters have strange&nbsp;
and twisted familial relationships.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:13.040
The authoritarian ruler of Apokolips known as&nbsp;
Darkseid is the son of the New Gods Yuga Khan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:17.120
and Heggra. While little is known of Yuga&nbsp;
Khan's family, Heggra has one known blood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:22.400
relative of note, and that's Steppenwolf. Yes,&nbsp;
this means that Steppenwolf is Darkseid's uncle.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:26.560
However, given that Darkseid is currently the&nbsp;
most powerful New God alive, Steppenwolf would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:30.320
be a fool to try and take the throne from&nbsp;
him, despite being his elder. Then again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:34.320
Steppenwolf seems to like his current&nbsp;
position as commander of Apokolips' military,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:38.400
since it lets him lead his forces into bloody&nbsp;
battle. He's been at it since the reign of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.960
Yuga Khan and Heggra, and since failure on&nbsp;
Apokolips is usually rewarded with death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:46.080
it's safe to say Steppenwolf must&nbsp;
be pretty good at what he does.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:50.400
Apokolips is one of the most&nbsp;
technologically-advanced planets in the DC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:55.200
Universe, and it likewise houses one of the most&nbsp;
powerful militaries. The only force that keeps the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:00.240
world in check and protects the rest of creation&nbsp;
is its antithesis, New Genesis. Since the planets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:04.800
are essentially oil and water, you might assume&nbsp;
they would wage an eternal war with one another.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:08.640
Once upon a time, they did — and&nbsp;
it was all Steppenwolf's fault.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:12.960
Ever since Darkseid was born, he schemed against&nbsp;
anyone who stood in his way to claim the throne of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:18.000
Apokolips. This included his parents, his older&nbsp;
brother Drax, and even his uncle Steppenwolf.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.000
While Drax was easy enough to eliminate,&nbsp;
Steppenwolf took a little more finesse. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:26.480
Darkseid convinced him to participate in the&nbsp;
popular Apokoliptian pastime of hunting down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:32.480
and killing New Genesisians. One of Steppenwolf's&nbsp;
targets was the leader of New Genesis, Highfather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:37.600
Izaya, but Izaya's wife, Avia, sacrificed&nbsp;
herself to save him. Following this, Izaya&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:42.880
slew Steppenwolf in revenge ... and thus, the war&nbsp;
between New Genesis and Apokolips was started.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:45.680
Of course, Darkseid eventually&nbsp;
resurrected Steppenwolf and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:49.920
put him in charge of the military of&nbsp;
Apokolips. Because, you know, comics.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:54.960
When Jack Kirby set about to create his New&nbsp;
Gods, he probably didn't realize just how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:59.520
big a role these godly beings would come to&nbsp;
play in the greater DC Universe. If he did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.200
he might not have killed off so many of&nbsp;
the major players so fast. Indeed, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:08.080
vast majority of Steppenwolf's appearances in Jack&nbsp;
Kirby-penned works happen in flashback sequences.

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:12.080
His New Gods #7 debut, wherein&nbsp;
Steppenwolf is temporarily killed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:16.080
was itself a flashback story. Though the&nbsp;
death obviously didn't take, the flashback&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:22.000
frame of New Gods #7 means that Steppenwolf was&nbsp;
functionally dead upon arrival in the DC Universe.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.680
Kirby's 1985 miniseries Super&nbsp;
Powers brought Steppenwolf back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:30.320
though this limited run was published as a toy&nbsp;
tie-in for Kenner Toys, and isn't considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:35.200
canon. It wasn't until DC published Who's Who:&nbsp;
The Definitive Directory of the DC Universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:39.280
that Steppenwolf's entry clarified it&nbsp;
was, indeed, Apokaliptian technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:42.880
administered by his nephew, Darkseid, that&nbsp;
brought him back to the world of the living.

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:48.000
The monstrous being known as Doomsday is known&nbsp;
for being unstoppable. But Steppenwolf somehow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:52.320
survived an encounter with this creature&nbsp;
long before Superman was even born. Even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:57.280
impressive is that — ever the loyal uncle — he&nbsp;
saved Darkseid from an early grave in the process.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:01.760
Darkseid's fight with Doomsday began thousands&nbsp;
of years ago, after the lord of Apokolips ran&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:07.200
afoul of Krypton's greatest monster, in a stroke&nbsp;
of cosmically bad luck. Doomsday hurtled himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:12.000
into the way like the organic, spikey meteor&nbsp;
he is, and he tore through Darkseid's forces.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.400
Even Darkseid almost fell before Doomsday's&nbsp;
unstoppable onslaught ... but Darkseid was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:20.640
rescued at the last minute by Steppenwolf.&nbsp;
However, since Darkseid's word is law,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:23.920
and it might be embarrassing to&nbsp;
acknowledge such a massive near-defeat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:27.360
he and Steppenwolf agreed to never&nbsp;
speak about this rescue ever again.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:31.040
In the DC Universe, Steppenwolf&nbsp;
is a part of ancient history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:35.840
and it's not entirely clear if Kirby ever expected&nbsp;
him to play such an active role in the present.

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:40.320
That changed a bit as the New Gods became&nbsp;
increasingly integrated with other stories.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:44.960
DC's New 52 initiative brought change to&nbsp;
just about every corner of the DC Universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:48.560
and despite Steppenwolf's god-like&nbsp;
abilities, he wasn't immune.

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:52.400
Among many, many other things, The&nbsp;
New 52 introduced a fresh version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:56.640
of Earth-2 to the DC multiverse. It's&nbsp;
there that Steppenwolf first staged a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:00.320
massive invasion of the blue planet&nbsp;
with a seething horde of parademons.

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:04.160
The ensuing battle results in some pretty&nbsp;
significant deaths on the hero side (but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:08.400
only the inferior Earth-2 versions), and&nbsp;
by the end, Steppenwolf decides to cool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:13.360
his heels in that universe, dodging a $300&nbsp;
million bounty on his head. Long story short,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:17.280
he's ultimately killed by Bizarro,&nbsp;
before later being resurrected yet again.

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:22.080
If you think the difference between Snyder's&nbsp;
Steppenwolf and the one in the 2017 version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:26.080
of Justice League is noticeable, well, his&nbsp;
comic book design has been all over the place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:30.000
and the iconic horned helmet he now&nbsp;
wears is a relatively recent addition.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.640
When Steppenwolf first appeared, for instance, his&nbsp;
look could be best described as "medieval-punk",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:39.040
with lime green skin, green armor and&nbsp;
belts, a green cape, and a pointy green hat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:43.120
that couldn't decide if it belonged to a&nbsp;
wizard or a pirate. This is the design that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:47.760
also made it to the DC Animated Universe, in&nbsp;
shows such as Superman: The Animated Series.

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:53.040
"Parademons, attack!"

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:59.680
However, for a time, comics abandoned&nbsp;
this classic look for one featuring an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:03.600
orange-red getup with spikes, and&nbsp;
a giant axe connected to his back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:07.120
This was part of the aforementioned&nbsp;
Super Powers Kenner toys tie-in.

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:10.320
The design went on to appear in&nbsp;
Mister Miracle comics and beyond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:14.480
and while subsequent DC cartoons returned&nbsp;
Steppenwolf to his original green threads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:17.440
the electro-axe became a mainstay of his armory.

